The contract, verified:

```tsx
// a component = a function from props to element(s)
function Badge({ tone, children }: { tone: 'good' | 'bad'; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <span className={tone === 'good' ? 'text-emerald-600' : 'text-rose-600'}>{children}</span>;
}

// usage — everything inside the tags becomes props.children
<Badge tone="good">Shipped ✓</Badge>
// props === { tone: 'good', children: <text>Shipped ✓</text> }

// composition: a shell that accepts ANY content as a slot
function Card({ title, actions, children }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <section className="rounded-lg border p-4">
      <header className="flex">
        <h3>{title}</h3>
        <div className="ml-auto">{actions}</div>   {/* slot 2: elements as props */}
      </header>
      {children}                                     {/* slot 1: the children prop */}
    </section>
  );
}
<Card title="Plan" actions={<Button>Upgrade</Button>}>
  <PriceTable />   {/* composed, not inherited */}
</Card>
```

The mental model (the interview core): render is a FUNCTION CALL TREE — React calls your component with props, your JSX calls more components, and the result is a tree of elements that React diffs. Two consequences: (1) props must be treated as immutable — the parent owns the values, and render purity (same props → same output) is what lets React skip or re-execute renders safely; (2) children is not special syntax, it's a prop — which means elements can be passed around, stored, conditioned: render the slot only when data loaded, or pass different trees for different states.

Composition over inheritance (the React design answer you should give): instead of a BaseCard class extended by PricingCard, build Card as a SHELL with slots (children, actions, footer as element props) and specialize by what you pass in. Specialization pattern: a Card that pre-fills its own props then forwards the rest: const SidebarCard = (p) => <Card tone="side" {...p} /> — two lines, no class hierarchy.

The typing layer (worth one line): props are a TS interface — children: React.ReactNode (NOT ReactElement — it must accept strings, numbers, fragments, arrays); when forwarding props, use ComponentProps<typeof X> to inherit rather than redeclare.
